<SiteLayout title="How to Convert Subtitles to Plain Text or TXT | ConvertAnyFile" description="Turn SRT, VTT, ASS, SSA, or SBV subtitles into readable plain text. Learn when to keep timestamps, what formatting is removed, and how to export locally in your browser.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Subtitle to plain text</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Subtitle guide</span><h1>How to convert subtitles to plain text</h1><p>TXT export is useful when you need the dialogue itself instead of a subtitle file: transcripts, notes, review copies, search, editing, or text analysis. The key decision is whether the output should keep timing context.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} relatedTitle="Related subtitle tools & guides">
<div class="quick-answer" id="quick-answer"><strong>Quick answer</strong><p>Open a subtitle-to-TXT route such as <a href="/srt-to-txt/">SRT to TXT</a>, add the subtitle file, choose whether TXT should contain no timestamps, start times, or full start → end ranges, then convert and download the readable text.</p></div>
<h2 id="why">Why export a subtitle as plain text?</h2><p>A timed-text format is designed for playback. A transcript is designed for reading and reuse. Exporting to TXT can make subtitles easier to paste into documents, search through, review for spelling, summarize, or move into another writing workflow.</p><p>The conversion does not perform speech-to-text. It uses text that already exists in the subtitle file.</p>
<h2 id="options">Should plain text include timestamps?</h2><p>That depends on what happens next. A clean transcript often needs no timestamps at all. Review workflows may benefit from a start time before each cue. Editing or localization notes can be easier when each line carries the complete start → end range.</p><ul><li><strong>No time:</strong> cleanest reading copy.</li><li><strong>Start time:</strong> useful for locating dialogue quickly.</li><li><strong>Start → end:</strong> keeps full cue timing context.</li></ul>
<h2 id="steps">How to export subtitles as TXT</h2><ol><li>Open the <a href="/subtitle-converter/">Subtitle Converter</a> or a direct route such as <a href="/srt-to-txt/">SRT to TXT</a>.</li><li>Add SRT, VTT, ASS, SSA, or SBV files.</li><li>Select TXT as the destination.</li><li>Choose the timestamp style and cue spacing you want.</li><li>Convert locally and download the text output.</li><li>Review the result before using it as a transcript or source document.</li></ol>
<h2 id="limits">What gets removed in plain-text export?</h2><p>TXT is intentionally not a subtitle presentation format. Inline markup is removed so the result stays readable. ASS/SSA styling, karaoke instructions, positioning, drawing commands, WebVTT regions, STYLE blocks, and other playback metadata do not belong in a plain-text transcript.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Keep the subtitle source</strong><p>TXT is a reading or editing copy. Keep the original subtitle if you may need timing, styling, or player metadata later.</p></div>
<h2 id="faq">Common subtitle-to-text questions</h2><h3>Can I remove all timestamps?</h3><p>Yes. Choose the TXT option with no timing information.</p><h3>Does TXT keep subtitle formatting?</h3><p>No. TXT export is plain readable text.</p><h3>Can ASS or SSA become plain text?</h3><p>Yes. Readable dialogue can be extracted while rich presentation instructions are removed.</p><h3>Does the tool translate the text?</h3><p>No. The language remains unchanged.</p>
